            <ArticleTitle>Single-needle arthrocentesis (Shepard cannula) vs. double-needle arthrocentesis for treating disc displacement without reduction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>296-302</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/08869634.2015.1106810</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare single- vs. double-needle arthrocentesis with viscosupplementation for treating disc displacement without reduction of the temporomandibular joint.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty-six patients with a magnetic resonance imaging diagnosis of non-reducing disc displacement were included. Arthrocentesis with viscosupplementation was performed on patients in group I using the Y-shaped needle (Shepard cannula) technique; the standard double-needle technique was performed on patients in group II. Patients were evaluated on postoperative day 1, and 1, 3, and 6 months later, patient- and procedure-related parameters were assessed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In both groups, significant improvement in the baseline levels was achieved (p &lt; 0.01). Both techniques were equally effective at reducing pain and increasing the maximal mouth opening. The single-needle technique was easier to perform and required a shorter operative time (p &lt; 0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Single-needle (Shepard cannula) arthrocentesis can be an alternative to the standard technique; however, it might add to the cost of the procedure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
